today I'm going to show you a cocktail  called the Tigre Blanco it was created  by Isaac Flores from dick and Janes in  Brooklyn this is a tequila based  cocktail so you're gonna need some of  that some Blanco tequila some fresh lime  fresh OJ triple sec some smoked chili  lime salt and you can buy at the store  and it's some horse shot dub so we're  going to use our first shot the first  two ounces right into the shaker  horchata is a traditional Mexican rice  milk that's been flavored with a variety  of spices typically it's cinnamon and  vanilla you can make this yourself just  by blending rice and water and letting  you sit for a few hours but a lot of  Mexican restaurants also make it so if  you have one you really like you I'm  going to ask them and maybe they'll cite  you some and now our fresh lime juice  just 1/4 ounce  okay half an ounce of OJ fresh orange  juice nothing pizza  now our triple sec ounce of triple sec  and finally an ounce and a half of  Blanco tequila  make sure the bottle says 100% agave on  it that means the only thing that's in  there is a ghave which is what it should  be a lot of tequila producers try to cut  corners by putting other kinds of grain  alcohols and other weird stuff in  tequila because agaves takes so long to  come to maturity sometimes eight to ten  years so just make sure it's 100% agave  on the bottle and you'll be good to go  okay so we got everything in here before  we shake though I'm going to rim our  glass with the chili lime salt rims are  a really cool way to add another  dimension of flavor to cocktails just  rub the edge with lime and dab it in  you've probably used to salt on a  margarita or something but you can use  all kinds of spices and seasonings make  your own mixtures black pepper works  lime zest I like a little sugar I think  it helps to boost the flavor it's a fun  thing to play around with okay so we're  going to throw some ice in our glass and  now it is time to shape we're going to  shake this one super button we're going  to do what's called a whip shake which  is just trying to get the rice milk to  foam up and give the drink really nice  frothy texture  see a really nice foam on top and  garnish with an orange wheel and that is  a tea grade Blanco this is a great  example of a cocktail it draws its  inspiration from food and it's also a  great example of a cocktail that pairs  really well with food so the next time  you're having a really really spicy meal  I recommend keeping us cocktail in mind  if you liked this video and you're  looking for more tips to step up your  bartending game subscribe to the GQ  channel we've got lots more where that  came from
 

